The shipped untyped_receiver caveat matches the called method's NAME against
primitives catalogued for the claimed boundary. A name lookup is exactly what an
untyped receiver makes meaningless, and the consequence was measured rather than
argued: a hunt across 32,593 non-test files found 90 real scopes whose untyped
receiver calls a genuine I/O verb outside the 120 catalogued method-kind names
(post 56, upload_file 6, upload 6, put_item/get_item 6, download_file 5,
execute_command 3). The existing caveat fired on ZERO of them.

Not one was protected by anything this module owns. All 90 were held back by a
COVERAGE GAP -- 68 uncatalogued-module, 13 opaque-launch, 5 unsupported-language
-- every one of which the project intends to close. The safety property was
anti-correlated with the tool improving.

REPRODUCED END-TO-END ON UNMODIFIED REAL CODE, not a fixture. polis
deploy/deploy-static-assets.py is a CLI whose purpose is uploading static assets
to S3:

    def upload_file(s3_client, file_path, bucket, base_path):   # UNANNOTATED
        s3_client.upload_file(str(file_path), bucket, relative_path, ...)
    s3_client = boto3.client("s3")

With a realistic project overlay that audits boto3 but omits upload_file from its
rows, the shipped CLI returned rc 0 and "never sends data over the network:
confirmed". The documented remedy for the OTHER half of INV-fibis -- project
overlays (INV-fotav) -- is exactly what removes the accidental protection for
this half. It now returns rc 3, naming upload_file among the methods it could
not adjudicate.

WHAT THIS ADDS: caveat kind `unknown_receiver_scope`, carrying a count, a
denominator and the distinct method names. A bare "some receivers were unknown"
is unactionable; 1-of-3 and 1-of-3000 are different verdicts, and append/items
reads very differently from upload_file/post (34.7% of untyped-receiver sites
across the corpus are builtin container/string names).

WHY THIS IS NOT THE DOWNGRADE REFUSED ON 2026-08-11. That proposal WITHHELD the
verdict -- inconclusive on every boundary of every repo. This QUALIFIES it: the
verdict still reads clean, rc moves 0 -> 3 (documented fail-closed), and it is
CAPABLE OF NOT FIRING -- an analysis whose receivers are all typed keeps a bare
confirmed at rc 0, pinned by test_all_typed_receivers_stays_bare_confirmed,
because a caveat that is always present is one its reader learns to discount.

TESTS THAT PINNED THE OLD DECISION WERE INVERTED, NOT DELETED.
TestTheResidualThisDoesNotClose asserted that session.post(...) "still confirms"
and carried the note "a PR that changes it should be closing INV-fibis, not
passing by". It is now TestTheResidualNowClosed and records the old argument in
full: its premise (there is no signal distinguishing session.post from
items.append) survives and is the finding; its conclusion (therefore stay
silent) does not, because a call on an object of unknown type is not evidence of
ABSENCE either, and must_not_exist is an assertion about absence. Three
boundary-scoping tests were NARROWED to the guarantee that actually survives --
no boundary-SPECIFIC claim is made about an unrelated .get -- rather than
weakened.

Consumer impact, plainly: claims that exited 0 may now exit 3. No envelope bump
(a new caveat kind is additive), but a gate written `verify-claims || exit 1`
fails where it passed. The four clean-verdict paths now build their caveat list
through ONE builder, so a future disclosure cannot be attached to three of them
and forgotten on the fourth.

